## State of this copy

- Formatted with the repository's `oxfmt`; `oxfmt --check`, `oxlint` and `pnpm typecheck` pass with
  the whole set applied.
- The fence's doc comment no longer refers to an outside design note, and its sentence on a
  takeover reads "can complete only inside the server's own commit" (the original said "cannot
  complete only in", the opposite of what it explains).
- Carries a test of the library half: `packages/integration-tests/test/Domain/CommitFence.test.ts`
  (a host that refuses a commit with `ShardLost` sees it retried on the owner), and the `fenced`
  option of the in-process host. It fails without this patch (no `ShardLost`), and fails with this
  patch minus the mapping in `commit` (the sender gets a `ShardLost` defect). The Postgres half
  (the `pg_locks` read, the deferred trigger and `AD001`) cannot run in process and has no test
  here; it ran on a two-runner Docker swarm with PostgreSQL 17.

## What changed from the original

- `DurableObjects.ts`: the hunk that turns a `ShardLost` defect into the call's failure now wraps
  the commit transaction of `main`'s restructured `commit`. The `ShardLost` class,
  `reportDefectsUnlessRefused` and the `send` wrapper are unchanged.
- `EffectCluster/index.ts`: unchanged apart from formatting and the doc comment above.
- Open, for a maintainer: on `main` the decision protocol (#3420) also writes `meta` inside storage
  transactions (`hold`, `applyIntent`, `release`, `txPrepare`). The fence's trigger covers those
  writes, so a runner that lost the shard cannot hold or apply a decision either; but there the
  refusal reaches the sender as a defect, not as a retried failure. In process (a probe, not one
  of the carried tests), nothing is recorded, both instances stay held, and a resend of the same
  command id on the owner finishes the decision once. A later command to a held instance should
  carry it on as well (`whenFree` settles a held instance first); that was read, not run.

The original description follows unchanged. Its line references are to 33c10dd.

fix(domain): on Effect Cluster, fence a commit on its runner's shard lock

Against alchemy `packages/domain/src` at 33c10dd (2.0.0-beta.87) with
`03-commit-version-check.patch` applied first. Apply from the repository root with `git apply`;
the text above the first `---` line is ignored.

## Problem

With 03, two runners holding one instance while its shard moves no longer lose an acknowledged
write: their commits serialize on the instance's advisory lock and the later one fails its version
check. But they are ordered by transaction, not by ownership. A runner that stalls inside its commit
(a GC pause, a partition, a frozen container) past its shard lock, while another runner takes the
shard and writes, is still acknowledged when it resumes, because its transaction began first.

How Effect Cluster 4.1.4 records ownership on PostgreSQL
(`packages/effect/src/cluster/SqlRunnerStorage.ts`, `Sharding.ts`):

- each shard is a session-level advisory lock,
  `pg_try_advisory_lock(namespace, (groupIndex + 1) * 1000000 + shard)`, with the namespace an
  FNV-1a hash of `effect-cluster:<prefix>`, held on one reserved connection per runner
  (`acquireLockConn`, `pgLocks`);
- that session's `idle_session_timeout` is the lock expiry (35 s by default), so a frozen runner's
  session ends and its locks are released;
- a runner is listed only while its heartbeat in `cluster_runners` is within the same expiry
  (`getRunners`), and shards are assigned over the listed runners (`Sharding.ts`, the hash ring);
  so 35 s into a freeze another runner is assigned the shards and takes their locks.

A domain commit runs on another connection (the domain's own pool). It cannot take or share the
shard's lock, whose holder is another session; it can read it in `pg_locks`.

## Fix

`objectStorage.transaction` fences every transaction, after the instance's advisory lock (03):

- first, it finds the session named after this runner that holds the instance's shard lock (see
  below), and records that session's pid in a transaction-local setting, `alchemy.commit_fence`;
  with none, the transaction dies with `ShardLost` before it writes;
- a deferred constraint trigger on the storage table (`AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE`,
  `WHEN (NEW.key = 'meta')`, `INITIALLY DEFERRED`) checks at `COMMIT` that the same pid still holds
  the lock, and otherwise raises SQLSTATE `AD001`, which aborts the commit; the storage reports it
  as `ShardLost`. The migration creates the trigger and its function, one runner at a time.
- `commit` turns `ShardLost` into the call's failure, as it does 03's `CommitConflict`
  (`reportDefectsUnlessRefused`): never acknowledged, retried by the caller under the same command
  id, starting from the receipt lookup.
- Each refusal is logged (`commit fenced: ...`).

The check at `COMMIT` runs after the last point where the runner itself can stall. A takeover can
complete only inside the server's own commit, between the trigger and the commit record; even then
the new owner cannot commit to the instance before this commit is visible (03's advisory lock), and
its own commit then fails 03's version check.

Which session is this runner's: the one named after it. 08 names the pool Effect reserves the lock
session from `effect-cluster:<host>:<port>`, from the address the runner gives Effect, and the fence
computes the same name from `ShardingConfig.runnerAddress` (`runnerPoolName`). There is no fallback
to the session's address or to any holder: behind a proxy or load balancer every session has the
same address, and taking another runner's session for this one's is how a stale runner gets
acknowledged.

## Why it is safe

- Durable Objects (Cloudflare) and Rivet are unchanged: the fence is in the Effect Cluster storage
  only, and their storage already admits one instance at a time. The in-memory platform is
  unchanged.
- A commit that is refused writes nothing, and its retry reaches the current owner: the same path
  as 03's `CommitConflict`.
- A runner whose pool is not named (a host that builds its own runner pool) has no session of its
  own, so every commit it makes is refused and retried until the caller gives up. That is the safe
  failure: it never acknowledges a write it cannot attribute. It is silent apart from the
  `commit fenced` lines; `10-fail-at-start-without-a-named-pool.patch` makes such a runner fail at
  start instead.

## Failing case, and how it was reproduced

- In process (two in-process runners on one instance's storage, the host fencing a transaction
  whose runner no longer serves the instance when it commits): runner 0 decides, the instance moves
  to runner 1, runner 0's commit returns with the version unchanged. Without the fence it is
  acknowledged; with it, it is refused and its retry commits on runner 1.
- On a cluster (two runners and a front on Effect Cluster with PostgreSQL 17, on a single-node Docker
swarm on one workstation), with 08: a runner frozen with `docker pause` inside its commit (held on a
  row lock from another session) until the other runner's named session holds its shard; one
  write sent at once, which waits in the frozen runner's queue, and another after the shard moved.
  In six runs the frozen runner's commit was refused at `COMMIT` every time, and every write the
  resumed runner decided before it took its shard back was refused at the fence's first read
  (`does not hold shard`). Each refused write was committed once, by whichever runner's own session
  held the shard at the time, the last 12.03 s after the unpause. A shard's holder was sampled by
  name every half second after the unpause. An earlier version of this fence, which fell back to
  matching sessions by client address, acknowledged such a write in three of five runs: every
  session there arrived from the swarm's load-balancer address.
- After a lock session is ended (`pg_terminate_backend`), the session Effect rebuilds carries the
  runner's name and takes the shards back within 15 s; no session other than the two named lock
  sessions held a lock in the cluster's namespace at any sample.

## Where it ran

- On a cluster: the paused-inside-commit case, six runs, and the rebuild probe, with 01 to 03, 06
  and 08 applied.
- In process: the fenced-commit case above.

## Depends on

03 (applied first; this extends its refusal of a commit) and 08 (`runnerPoolName`, in
`packages/effect-cluster`, needed to compile). No hunk overlaps 01, 02 or 06. The fence's doc
comment carries a stray reference to an outside design note; 10 removes it.
